Let $\Gamma$ be a finite tree with vertex set $V$ and at least one edge, carrying its graph path metric $d_\Gamma$ ([[def-graph-path-metric]], [[thm-the-path-metric-of-a-connected-simple-graph-is-a-metric]]). Give each edge the length $1$ and form the **interval realization** $X_\Gamma$: a compact convex $1$-cell $[0,1]$ for each edge, glued at endpoints according to the incidence of $\Gamma$, with the chain metric $d$ of [[def-cg-abstract-isometric-polyhedral-gluing-and-chain-metric]]. Then $X_\Gamma$ is connected, locally finite and has finitely many shapes, so $(X_\Gamma,d)$ is complete by [[thm-cg-polyhedral-chain-metric-topology-and-properness]] and is geodesic by the verification below; moreover:

**(i)** on the vertex set $V$, the chain metric restricts to the graph metric: $d(v,w)=d_\Gamma(v,w)$ for all $v,w\in V$, both equal to the number of edges of the unique $\Gamma$-path from $v$ to $w$;

**(ii)** every two points of $X_\Gamma$ are joined by exactly one geodesic segment ([[def-geodesic-and-geodesic-metric-space]]), and the midpoints of edges are points of $X_\Gamma$ at distance $1/2$ from their endpoints, so the intrinsic metric takes non-integer values on points that the discrete metric does not see;

**(iii)** the vertex set with its graph metric is **not** geodesic when $\Gamma$ has an edge: $d_\Gamma$ is integer-valued on distinct vertices, so no point lies at distance $1/2$ from a vertex;

**(iv)** if the edges are given prescribed lengths $\ell_e>0$ instead of $1$, then the chain metric still restricts on $V$ to the weighted path length $\sum_{e\in\mathrm{path}(v,w)}\ell_e$, which equals the unweighted graph distance $d_\Gamma$ only when every $\ell_e=1$; for instance on a single edge of length $2$ the two distances are $2$ and $1$.

Thus the discrete vertex metric is not a substitute for the interval-realized intrinsic metric: it is defined on a different set, it ignores edge lengths, and it is not geodesic.

**Statement refuted.** "Every connected locally finite isometric polyhedral gluing whose cells are compact and convex, with no hypothesis on the number of isometry classes of cells, is complete for its chain metric."

**Counterexample.** For $n\in\mathbb N$ let $C_n$ be a closed interval of length $2^{-n}$; glue $C_n$ to $C_{n+1}$ by identifying the right endpoint of the former with the left endpoint of the latter, and include the one-point cells at the endpoints, and include the one-point cells at the endpoints, and let $X$ be the resulting one-dimensional isometric polyhedral gluing (the **shrinking-edge ray**). Then:

(i) each $C_n$ is a compact convex $1$-cell and the gluing satisfies the intersection condition, so $X$ is an isometric polyhedral gluing; its chain metric candidate $d$ is finite, symmetric and satisfies the triangle inequality;

(ii) $X$ is connected and locally finite: every point of $X$ lies in at most three cells (including the vertex point cells);

(iii) $(X,d)$ is isometric to the half-open interval $[0,2)$ with its Euclidean metric: the far endpoint $p_k$ of $C_k$ lies at distance $s_k=\sum_{i=0}^{k}2^{-i}=2-2^{-k}$ from the initial endpoint, and these coordinates are additive and compatible with the chain metric;

(iv) nevertheless $(X,d)$ is not complete ([[def-complete-metric-space]]): the sequence $(p_k)$ of far endpoints is Cauchy ([[def-cauchy-in-metric]]), since $d(p_k,p_m)=2^{-k}-2^{-m}<2^{-k}$ for $m>k$, but it has no limit, because every point $p$ of $X$ lies in some cell $C_N$, hence has coordinate at most $2-2^{-N}$, while $p_k$ has coordinate $2-2^{-k}$, so $d(p,p_k)\ge 2^{-N}-2^{-k}\ge 2^{-(N+1)}$ for every $k>N$; equivalently $[0,2)$ is not complete ([[def-metric-convergence]]);

(v) the hypothesis (H3) of finite shapes fails: the cells $C_n$ have pairwise distinct isometry classes. Hence the counterexample pinpoints the dropped hypothesis: local finiteness and compactness of the cells do not replace finitely many shapes, exactly as asserted in [[thm-cg-polyhedral-chain-metric-topology-and-properness]]. The space is also not proper: its closed ball of radius $2$ about the initial endpoint is all of $X$, which is not compact.
